{"type":"message_update","assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"thinking_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"Now let me also add a safety pre-check for stale temp Go test file before writing (in case of a previous crashed run). Actually, my `finally` handles it, and `open(..., 'w')` overwrites. But if the file somehow exists as a stale file from a crashed run, my write overwrites it, then finally removes it. That's fine. But there's a subtle issue: what if a stale file exists and my script's runtime test section is reached, I overwrite it, run the test, and finally removes it. Fine. No stale file remains.\n\nHowever, there's one more subtle scenario: if the FULL go test guard runs while a stale file exists (from a PREVIOUS crashed run of THIS script), the guard would compile the stale test and it would fail (since Run still doesn't error). But that's actually the correct RED behavior anyway, and my script's flow would have overwritten+removed it in the runtime test section BEFORE the guard. The only way a stale file exists at guard time is if the runtime test section was skipped (e.g., internal/runtime dir doesn't exist). In that case I already error. Edge case, acceptable.\n\nLet me add a defensive pre-removal before writing to be safe. Actually, it's fine as-is. Let me not over-engineer.\n\nLet me re-run the test to confirm the SyntaxWarning is gone and the output is cleaner."}}
