temporary Go test file INTO the repo's `internal/runtime/` directory (e.g., `internal/runtime/task8_verify_test.go`), run `go test ./internal/runtime/... -run TestTask8Verify`, then remove the file. This test would be in package `runtime` (same package), so it can access `NewPiRuntime` and `Run` and also unexported stuff. This is a clean way to test real behavior.\n\nBut wait — is it appropriate for the Python test script to write a Go file into the repo, run it, and remove it? It's idempotent if I use a unique filename and clean up in finally. But it modifies the repo temporarily. The task7 test didn't do this. However, it's a legitimate approach to test real behavior.\n\nActually, even simpler and cleaner: I can do a source-level check combined with running the existing `go test ./internal/runtime/...`. The runtime package's own tests (added in task8) should cover the \"Run returns error on missing agent_settled\" case. Since I can't rely on a specific test name, I'll do:\n\n1. Source-level: `runtime.go` `Run` method enforces readiness (contains `isSessionReady` call). This is the key structural signal.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The cleanest, most robust, and still-RED approach:\n\nWrite a temporary Go test file in `internal/runtime/` that:\n- Creates a fake pi binary (shell script) emitting JSONL WITHOUT agent_settled.\n- Calls `NewPiRuntime(fakeBin).Run(...)`.\n- Asserts that err != nil (since task8 requires \"未收 agent_settled 返回 error\").\n\nThis tests REAL behavior (the actual `Run` method + `parseStream`), uses a real (fake) pi subprocess, and is exactly what the 测试方法 describes (\"runtime `Run` 在 fake JSONL 缺 `agent_settled` 时返回 error\").\n\nThis will fail now (current Run returns nil error even without agent_settled) and pass after task8. This is a great RED assertion.\n\nLet me design this carefully. The fake pi script emits:\n```\n{\"type\":\"session\",\"id\":\"s123\"}\n```\n(no agent_settled). Then `Run` should return error.\n\nCurrently, `Run` call